1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,695
Friday 18:10. Gate’s new Boot jar is
“feature complete” in the demo
environment. Trucks are already queued for
the weekend shift. Kubernetes rolls the
Deployment.

2
00:00:12,815 --> 00:00:21,863
Within minutes pods restart in a loop, the
Service has no ready endpoints, and the
booth sees timeouts. Nobody changed the
release logic.

3
00:00:21,983 --> 00:00:34,295
The jar was not ready for production as an
operating problem — probes, startup cost,
shutdown, and a missing runbook — even
though the feature demo looked fine.
Production readiness is that operating
problem.

4
00:00:34,415 --> 00:00:47,783
Spring Boot Actuator, Micrometer, and
graceful shutdown are tools inside it.
They are not a checklist that replaces
judgment about how this service fails in
front of real traffic. Start with the
Friday failure.

5
00:00:47,903 --> 00:00:53,951
Liveness and readiness both pointed at a
deep health indicator that pinged billing
and the ledger database.

6
00:00:54,071 --> 00:01:07,319
A brief billing blip marked the pod not-
live; Kubernetes killed it; startup ran
Flyway again; the next blip killed it
again. Symptom: restart loop during a
dependency hiccup while the JVM process
itself was fine.

7
00:01:07,439 --> 00:01:15,408
Fix the meaning of the probes. Liveness
should answer “should the platform kill
me?” — cheap process health.

8
00:01:15,527 --> 00:01:28,823
Readiness should answer “should I receive
traffic?” — including critical
dependencies you are unwilling to serve
without. Startup probes give Flyway and
warm caches time before liveness is
allowed to kill a slow boot.

9
00:01:28,943 --> 00:01:42,647
Look at this example. management: Look at
this example. livenessProbe: Readiness can
remove the pod from Service endpoints
while the DB is down without restarting
it. Liveness stays cheap.

10
00:01:42,767 --> 00:01:51,047
If both probes mean “deep dependency
check,” you recreate Friday’s loop. The
same go-live has to survive deploy
mechanics.

11
00:01:51,167 --> 00:01:59,255
Migrations belong in the pipeline with
clear ownership — a failed migration fails
the rollout instead of leaving half the
fleet on schema V41.

12
00:01:59,375 --> 00:02:09,383
Observability is not optional decoration:
if releaseGate has no timer, Prometheus
has no scrape, and traces do not cover
check-in, the first incident has no
baseline.

13
00:02:09,503 --> 00:02:21,575
Resilience settings you already learned —
timeouts, breakers, bounded caches — are
readiness items when the booth depends on
them. And shutdown matters: when a pod
drains, in-flight releases should finish.

14
00:02:21,695 --> 00:02:33,815
Look at this example. server: Walk a
drain. SIGTERM arrives; Boot stops
accepting new work; in-flight releaseGate
may finish within the timeout; then the
process exits.

15
00:02:33,935 --> 00:02:46,463
Too short and you cut ledger writes mid-
transaction. Too long and deploys stall.
Align with kube
terminationGracePeriodSeconds. Runbooks
are part of the artifact.

16
00:02:46,583 --> 00:02:57,359
When billingQuote is open, who is paged,
what fallback the booth uses, how recovery
is verified — including the Grafana link
and the breaker metric name — must not
live only in Slack history.

17
00:02:57,479 --> 00:03:03,359
Rollback for a bad tariff ConfigMap or a
bad image belongs next to the forward
deploy steps.

18
00:03:03,479 --> 00:03:13,367
A practical readiness test: someone
unfamiliar with the service follows the
runbook for “billing breaker open” and
“rollback tariff ConfigMap” using only
links in the artifact.

19
00:03:13,487 --> 00:03:19,343
If they need tribal knowledge, you are not
ready. Trade-offs stay explicit.

20
00:03:19,463 --> 00:03:29,615
Deep readiness checks catch dependency
loss early and couple your traffic shape
to that dependency’s blips — sometimes
degraded mode (accept check-in, queue
invoice) beats going NotReady.

21
00:03:29,735 --> 00:03:34,055
Document which choice the quay wants
before night shift invents one.

22
00:03:34,175 --> 00:03:42,551
Another trap: equating “Deployed to prod”
with readiness while probes stay UP and
the ledger is read-only broken because
health never checked what the booth needs.

23
00:03:42,671 --> 00:03:46,991
A third: disabling noisy probes instead of
fixing the check.

24
00:03:47,111 --> 00:03:58,415
Once a service can survive production
operations, a different pressure appears:
the codebase itself gets harder to change
— controllers talk to SQL, domain rules
scatter, every feature touches everything.

25
00:03:58,535 --> 00:04:02,663
Architecture boundaries become the next
operating concern.
